  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "confidence": 0.6,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "bridged_nets",
      "components": ["U4"],
      "pins": ["U4.1", "U4.2"],
      "nets": ["+5V", "VCC"],
      "observed": "On the sheet (right-hand tile, row A), the LM1117-5.0 regulator U4 has both its input pin (VI, pin 1) and its output pin (VO, pin 2) connected to the same +5V net: the VI wire runs left to the node that carries the +5V arrow and input/output cap C1, and the VO wire runs right to a +5V arrow. The actual raw supply net VCC (from J3 PWR pin 2, and the decoupling cap C3) is never connected to U4's input.",
      "expected": "A fixed 5 V linear regulator must have its VIN pin tied to the higher input rail (VCC) and its VOUT pin tied to the regulated +5V rail. Tying VIN directly to VOUT bypasses the regulator (a 5 V LM1117 cannot regulate a 5 V input, and the input and output nets are shorted together), so the +5V rail is not derived from VCC and the regulator does nothing.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "U4 LM1117-5.0: pin 1 'VI' wired to the +5V/C1 node; pin 2 'VO' wired to a +5V arrow (sheet /, right tile, row A)"},
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "J3 PWR pin 2 -> VCC; C3 100nF VCC-GND; VCC also feeds R7, JP2 and Q2 source, but no wire from VCC to U4"},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "'an ATtiny-driven fan controller' (Atmel/ category) - regulator must step the input rail down to the MCU's +5V"}
      ],
      "impact": "The 5 V logic rail is not produced from the VCC supply rail; if the board is powered only through the VCC input (J3), the ATtiny receives no regulated 5 V and the fan controller will not run. The regulator's input and output are effectively shorted, so it provides no regulation or protection.",
      "confidence": 0.6
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The schematic appears to miswire the LM1117-5.0 regulator U4: its input (VI) is tied to the +5V output net instead of the VCC input rail, shorting input to output and leaving VCC disconnected from the regulator, so the 5V rail is never derived from the supply. This is the only deviation I found that the drawing clearly supports."
}

